1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:02,942
It's February. Your gate is shut.

2
00:00:03,127 --> 00:00:04,297
The yard is mud,

3
00:00:04,317 --> 00:00:05,787
and the phone is quiet.

4
00:00:06,083 --> 00:00:08,034
But in Barnsley, England,

5
00:00:08,054 --> 00:00:13,016
families pay thirteen pounds ninety-five
each to walk into a farm in that same cold

6
00:00:13,036 --> 00:00:15,394
month, for a chance to see a lamb born.

7
00:00:16,483 --> 00:00:18,681
Same month. Same mud.

8
00:00:18,802 --> 00:00:20,339
One farm is empty.

9
00:00:20,434 --> 00:00:22,268
The other is selling tickets.

10
00:00:22,583 --> 00:00:26,203
In this video, we will walk
through thirty farm event ideas,

11
00:00:26,223 --> 00:00:27,467
season by season,

12
00:00:27,487 --> 00:00:31,168
with the real ticket price a
real farm charged for each one.

13
00:00:31,483 --> 00:00:33,057
You don't need all thirty.

14
00:00:33,256 --> 00:00:36,402
You need one for each stretch of
the year when your gate sits shut.

15
00:00:36,717 --> 00:00:38,345
And stay for number eleven.

16
00:00:38,488 --> 00:00:39,950
It's a one-hour class,

17
00:00:39,970 --> 00:00:41,229
and it sold out.

18
00:00:41,517 --> 00:00:44,491
I'm Dot. Yes, I'm a cartoon.

19
00:00:44,548 --> 00:00:46,726
And this is Agritourism Success.

20
00:00:47,017 --> 00:00:48,308
You're farming right.

21
00:00:48,435 --> 00:00:50,242
Nothing is wrong with your work.

22
00:00:50,361 --> 00:00:52,213
What's broken is the calendar.

23
00:00:52,517 --> 00:00:53,991
The old way is simple.

24
00:00:54,135 --> 00:00:56,046
You open when the crop is ready.

25
00:00:56,173 --> 00:00:59,598
A few busy weeks, then
a long, quiet nothing.

26
00:00:59,883 --> 00:01:01,306
The new way flips it.

27
00:01:01,385 --> 00:01:03,609
You open when the calendar is empty.

28
00:01:03,763 --> 00:01:05,573
We call it the Twelve-Month Gate.

29
00:01:05,883 --> 00:01:10,529
Here's the rule. Every season gets one
paid reason to come through your gate.

30
00:01:10,817 --> 00:01:12,229
And people do pay.

31
00:01:12,403 --> 00:01:17,871
American farms and ranches earned one point
two six billion dollars from agritourism

32
00:01:17,891 --> 00:01:19,513
in twenty twenty-two.

33
00:01:19,586 --> 00:01:22,051
That's up more than twelve
percent in five years,

34
00:01:22,071 --> 00:01:23,403
after inflation.

35
00:01:23,717 --> 00:01:27,042
About twenty-eight thousand six
hundred farms earned that money,

36
00:01:27,062 --> 00:01:30,831
averaging forty-four thousand
dollars each, before costs.

37
00:01:30,977 --> 00:01:35,503
That's only about one and a half percent
of American farms. So there's room.

38
00:01:35,817 --> 00:01:37,765
Now, what this is not.

39
00:01:37,891 --> 00:01:39,855
I'm not telling you to quit farming.

40
00:01:40,036 --> 00:01:44,698
And this is not thirty things to
do this year. Pick one. Just one.

41
00:01:44,983 --> 00:01:48,325
One more thing. A ticket
price is not profit.

42
00:01:48,423 --> 00:01:50,139
These are what farms charged.

43
00:01:50,212 --> 00:01:51,910
Your costs are yours to count,

44
00:01:51,930 --> 00:01:53,839
and we'll do that math near the end.

45
00:01:54,850 --> 00:01:57,286
Winter. Your quietest months,

46
00:01:57,306 --> 00:01:58,578
so we start here.

47
00:01:58,883 --> 00:02:01,978
Number one. Choose-and-cut
Christmas trees.

48
00:02:02,130 --> 00:02:04,287
Hill's Christmas Tree Farm in Springville,

49
00:02:04,307 --> 00:02:06,434
New York charged ten dollars a foot,

50
00:02:06,454 --> 00:02:08,194
and opened just nine days.

51
00:02:08,483 --> 00:02:10,983
Number two. A reindeer barn.

52
00:02:11,048 --> 00:02:14,688
Cotley Farm in Devon charges
six pounds fifty per car,

53
00:02:14,708 --> 00:02:17,046
not per person. Buy a tree,

54
00:02:17,066 --> 00:02:18,390
and you get it back.

55
00:02:18,683 --> 00:02:21,469
Number three. A holiday light walk.

56
00:02:21,618 --> 00:02:26,992
Holland Ridge Farms in New Jersey charges
seventeen to twenty dollars a person to

57
00:02:27,012 --> 00:02:30,058
stroll past eighty thousand lit-up tulips.

58
00:02:30,350 --> 00:02:33,012
Number four. Santa on the farm.

59
00:02:33,204 --> 00:02:38,263
A special needs session at Fobbing Farm
Shop in Essex is fourteen pounds ninety-five

60
00:02:38,283 --> 00:02:39,138
per child.

61
00:02:39,450 --> 00:02:42,395
Number five. Dinner in a heated tent.

62
00:02:42,467 --> 00:02:44,222
Farm Club near Traverse City,

63
00:02:44,242 --> 00:02:48,193
Michigan charged fifty-five
dollars a person, drinks extra.

64
00:02:48,326 --> 00:02:49,827
January sold out.

65
00:02:50,117 --> 00:02:52,796
Number six. A snowdrop walk.

66
00:02:52,942 --> 00:02:57,811
South Ormsby Estate in Lincolnshire
charged five pounds for two guided miles,

67
00:02:57,831 --> 00:03:00,349
to look at small white
flowers in February.

68
00:03:00,650 --> 00:03:03,346
Number seven. A Valentine's dinner.

69
00:03:03,432 --> 00:03:05,286
Heritage Prairie Farm in Elburn,

70
00:03:05,306 --> 00:03:09,516
Illinois charged one hundred thirty
dollars a person for seven courses,

71
00:03:09,536 --> 00:03:11,360
with tax and tip included.

72
00:03:11,650 --> 00:03:15,475
Number eight. The lambing
festival at Cannon Hall Farm.

73
00:03:15,573 --> 00:03:18,817
Thirteen pounds ninety-five.
Hold that thought.

74
00:03:18,871 --> 00:03:20,348
We're coming back to them.

75
00:03:21,350 --> 00:03:23,587
Spring. The mud is still here,

76
00:03:23,607 --> 00:03:25,811
and so is your first chance of the year.

77
00:03:26,117 --> 00:03:29,230
Number nine. A maple pancake breakfast.

78
00:03:29,344 --> 00:03:31,792
Parker Family Maple Farm in West Chazy,

79
00:03:31,812 --> 00:03:36,654
New York charged sixteen dollars an
adult and ten a child. Cash only.

80
00:03:36,950 --> 00:03:39,452
Number ten. A pruning class.

81
00:03:39,605 --> 00:03:42,604
McDougal Orchards in Maine
charged fifty dollars,

82
00:03:42,624 --> 00:03:44,028
limit ten people.

83
00:03:44,156 --> 00:03:45,411
You tag your tree,

84
00:03:45,431 --> 00:03:47,677
and you come back in
the fall to check on it.

85
00:03:47,983 --> 00:03:50,613
Number eleven. The one I promised.

86
00:03:51,163 --> 00:03:52,722
A seed-starting class.

87
00:03:52,912 --> 00:03:56,203
Bedford Farms in New York charged
fifty dollars for one hour,

88
00:03:56,223 --> 00:03:59,186
soil and seeds included. It sold out.

89
00:03:59,483 --> 00:04:03,447
Think about what that asks
of you. A table. Some soil.

90
00:04:03,619 --> 00:04:05,070
One hour in March.

91
00:04:05,383 --> 00:04:08,000
Number twelve. Hands-on lambing.

92
00:04:08,168 --> 00:04:13,310
Lennox of Lomond in Scotland sold sessions
from twenty-five pounds up to one hundred

93
00:04:13,330 --> 00:04:15,129
ninety-five for a full day,

94
00:04:15,149 --> 00:04:17,575
with six spaces and a farmhouse lunch.

95
00:04:17,734 --> 00:04:19,921
People paid to work your hardest week.

96
00:04:20,217 --> 00:04:22,737
Number thirteen. Goat cuddles.

97
00:04:22,841 --> 00:04:26,585
Lunan Bay Farm in Angus
charged seventeen pounds fifty.

98
00:04:26,662 --> 00:04:28,070
The goats wear coats.

99
00:04:28,383 --> 00:04:29,696
Number fourteen.

100
00:04:29,888 --> 00:04:31,350
An Easter egg hunt.

101
00:04:31,486 --> 00:04:33,361
Sheldon Creek Dairy in Loretto,

102
00:04:33,381 --> 00:04:36,436
Ontario charged seven
Canadian dollars per child,

103
00:04:36,456 --> 00:04:37,708
with adults free.

104
00:04:38,017 --> 00:04:39,380
Number fifteen.

105
00:04:39,496 --> 00:04:40,780
U-pick tulips.

106
00:04:40,977 --> 00:04:42,235
Holland Ridge again,

107
00:04:42,255 --> 00:04:44,564
twenty to twenty-five dollars at the gate,

108
00:04:44,584 --> 00:04:46,806
and up to a dollar more for every stem.

109
00:04:47,117 --> 00:04:48,476
Number sixteen.

110
00:04:48,638 --> 00:04:49,974
Sheep shearing day.

111
00:04:50,111 --> 00:04:52,136
Forest Home Farms in San Ramon,

112
00:04:52,156 --> 00:04:54,460
California charged ten dollars early,

113
00:04:54,480 --> 00:04:57,315
and fifteen at the
gate. If you keep sheep,

114
00:04:57,335 --> 00:04:58,789
you were shearing anyway.

115
00:04:59,083 --> 00:05:00,772
Back to Cannon Hall Farm.

116
00:05:00,887 --> 00:05:02,238
By nineteen eighty-four,

117
00:05:02,258 --> 00:05:04,171
this farm was well into the red.

118
00:05:04,364 --> 00:05:06,609
The bank told the
Nicholson family to sell,

119
00:05:06,629 --> 00:05:08,374
while there was still a little left.

120
00:05:09,483 --> 00:05:11,014
So they sacked the banker.

121
00:05:11,317 --> 00:05:12,855
But brave wasn't enough.

122
00:05:13,012 --> 00:05:14,646
"We nearly went out of business,"

123
00:05:14,666 --> 00:05:17,267
Robert Nicholson told the Yorkshire Post.

124
00:05:17,463 --> 00:05:20,206
They were too small a beef
and sheep farm to make it pay.

125
00:05:20,517 --> 00:05:24,187
Therefore they sold three converted
farm buildings to buy time.

126
00:05:24,297 --> 00:05:25,593
And on Good Friday,

127
00:05:25,613 --> 00:05:26,801
nineteen eighty-nine,

128
00:05:26,821 --> 00:05:28,742
they opened the gate to visitors.

129
00:05:29,050 --> 00:05:31,769
Picture it. Twenty-five years later,

130
00:05:31,789 --> 00:05:34,982
that same farm was set to
turn over five million pounds,

131
00:05:35,002 --> 00:05:37,991
with thirty thousand schoolchildren
a year walking through.

132
00:05:38,283 --> 00:05:42,203
The farm the bank wanted sold
now sells tickets in February.

133
00:05:42,517 --> 00:05:45,337
If you're wondering whether
your own farm could do this,

134
00:05:45,357 --> 00:05:46,709
there's a free quiz.

135
00:05:46,857 --> 00:05:48,730
The link is in the description.

136
00:05:48,826 --> 00:05:51,023
And if this helps, subscribe,

137
00:05:51,043 --> 00:05:52,628
so the next one finds you.

138
00:05:53,617 --> 00:05:55,442
Summer. You're busy,

139
00:05:55,462 --> 00:05:56,659
but evenings are long,

140
00:05:56,679 --> 00:05:58,350
and town people want out.

141
00:05:58,650 --> 00:06:01,370
Number seventeen. Goat yoga.

142
00:06:01,542 --> 00:06:04,657
Maybury Farm in Michigan
charges thirty-five dollars,

143
00:06:04,677 --> 00:06:06,437
for up to twenty-five people.

144
00:06:06,504 --> 00:06:07,971
Thirty minutes of yoga,

145
00:06:07,991 --> 00:06:09,858
then fifteen of goat photos.

146
00:06:10,150 --> 00:06:12,832
Number eighteen. Flower fields.

147
00:06:12,960 --> 00:06:15,225
Kuipers Family Farm in Maple Park,

148
00:06:15,245 --> 00:06:17,770
Illinois charged eleven
ninety-nine a person,

149
00:06:17,790 --> 00:06:21,021
and nineteen ninety-nine more
for a bucket to cut your own.

150
00:06:21,317 --> 00:06:22,571
Number nineteen.

151
00:06:22,624 --> 00:06:23,951
A lavender walk.

152
00:06:24,038 --> 00:06:28,041
Cotswold Lavender charged seven
pounds seventy-five at peak bloom,

153
00:06:28,061 --> 00:06:31,679
and five seventy-five once
it passed. Notice that.

154
00:06:31,814 --> 00:06:33,741
The price moves with the flowers.

155
00:06:34,050 --> 00:06:35,322
Number twenty.

156
00:06:35,460 --> 00:06:36,712
Farm day camp.

157
00:06:36,787 --> 00:06:41,683
Underwood Family Farms in California charged
three hundred seventy-five dollars per

158
00:06:41,703 --> 00:06:44,427
child, per week. Mornings only.

159
00:06:44,717 --> 00:06:46,082
Number twenty-one.

160
00:06:46,272 --> 00:06:47,943
A sunset flower pick.

161
00:06:48,074 --> 00:06:49,927
Stony Hill Farms in Chester,

162
00:06:49,947 --> 00:06:53,052
New Jersey charged forty
dollars for a bucket of blooms,

163
00:06:53,072 --> 00:06:54,768
a hayride and one drink.

164
00:06:55,083 --> 00:06:56,505
Number twenty-two.

165
00:06:56,584 --> 00:06:58,220
A beekeeping experience.

166
00:06:58,356 --> 00:07:02,984
Mike and Niki's Honey in California
charges forty-five dollars to taste,

167
00:07:03,004 --> 00:07:04,896
and ninety-five to wear the suit.

168
00:07:05,183 --> 00:07:06,565
Number twenty-three.

169
00:07:06,759 --> 00:07:08,315
A cheese-making course.

170
00:07:08,493 --> 00:07:13,193
The Ethical Dairy in Scotland charges one
hundred five pounds for six and a half

171
00:07:13,213 --> 00:07:16,087
hours, and you carry home
about a kilo of cheese.

172
00:07:17,083 --> 00:07:19,188
Fall. You know this one.

173
00:07:19,255 --> 00:07:21,688
But look at how many ways
there are to sell it.

174
00:07:21,983 --> 00:07:23,347
Number twenty-four.

175
00:07:23,517 --> 00:07:24,933
A stargazing night.

176
00:07:25,030 --> 00:07:28,746
Hickory Nut Gap Farm in North
Carolina charged twelve dollars,

177
00:07:28,766 --> 00:07:30,282
led by an astronomer.

178
00:07:30,452 --> 00:07:31,944
The stars come free.

179
00:07:32,250 --> 00:07:35,089
Number twenty-five. A corn maze.

180
00:07:35,271 --> 00:07:38,500
Buford Corn Maze in Georgia
charges eighteen fifty-two,

181
00:07:38,520 --> 00:07:40,210
with unlimited hayrides.

182
00:07:40,517 --> 00:07:41,976
Number twenty-six.

183
00:07:42,122 --> 00:07:43,447
A pumpkin patch.

184
00:07:43,588 --> 00:07:47,578
Vala's in Nebraska charges
twenty-six ninety-nine on a weekday,

185
00:07:47,598 --> 00:07:51,155
and forty-five ninety-nine
on a weekend. Same patch.

186
00:07:51,300 --> 00:07:53,054
Nineteen dollars' difference.

187
00:07:53,350 --> 00:07:54,833
Number twenty-seven.

188
00:07:55,003 --> 00:07:56,390
A haunted hayride.

189
00:07:56,489 --> 00:08:01,143
Farm of Horrors at Schaefer Farms in
New Jersey starts at thirty-six dollars,

190
00:08:01,163 --> 00:08:03,021
for four haunts on one ticket.

191
00:08:03,317 --> 00:08:04,680
Number twenty-eight.

192
00:08:04,864 --> 00:08:06,126
A harvest dinner.

193
00:08:06,200 --> 00:08:10,616
Crieff Hills in Ontario charged ninety
Canadian dollars for a farm tour,

194
00:08:10,636 --> 00:08:12,317
four courses and a drink.

195
00:08:12,617 --> 00:08:14,016
Number twenty-nine.

196
00:08:14,099 --> 00:08:15,412
A full moon hike.

197
00:08:15,546 --> 00:08:19,517
Pineland Farms in Maine
charges ten dollars. Two miles,

198
00:08:19,537 --> 00:08:20,792
then a firepit.

199
00:08:21,083 --> 00:08:22,352
And number thirty.

200
00:08:22,471 --> 00:08:24,265
A wreath workshop with cows.

201
00:08:24,413 --> 00:08:27,631
Baylham House Farm in
Suffolk charges sixty pounds.

202
00:08:27,696 --> 00:08:29,094
You groom a Highland cow,

203
00:08:29,114 --> 00:08:30,524
then you make a wreath.

204
00:08:30,817 --> 00:08:33,620
That's thirty. Now the math I promised,

205
00:08:33,640 --> 00:08:36,012
because a full field can still lose money.

206
00:08:36,317 --> 00:08:38,795
Take a sample farm
dinner from our article.

207
00:08:38,901 --> 00:08:44,139
Forty-eight seats at one hundred fifty
dollars. The chef, staff, permits,

208
00:08:44,159 --> 00:08:47,775
lights and toilets come to three
thousand one hundred fifty dollars,

209
00:08:47,795 --> 00:08:49,484
before anyone sits down.

210
00:08:49,546 --> 00:08:52,159
Then each guest costs
about fifty-seven more.

211
00:08:52,450 --> 00:08:55,213
You need thirty-four
guests just to break even.

212
00:08:55,350 --> 00:08:56,628
Sell all forty-eight,

213
00:08:56,648 --> 00:08:59,243
and you keep about one
thousand three hundred dollars,

214
00:08:59,263 --> 00:09:00,769
before paying yourself.

215
00:09:00,934 --> 00:09:03,191
A dinner is big money and big risk.

216
00:09:03,483 --> 00:09:05,749
Now the class. Two classes,

217
00:09:05,769 --> 00:09:06,922
ten people each,

218
00:09:06,942 --> 00:09:08,235
fifty dollars.

219
00:09:08,421 --> 00:09:11,971
That's your first thousand
dollars at the gate, before costs.

220
00:09:12,067 --> 00:09:14,177
Small is a fine place to start.

221
00:09:14,483 --> 00:09:18,036
But my farm is too small,
you say. Look again.

222
00:09:18,151 --> 00:09:20,309
McDougal's class took ten people.

223
00:09:20,492 --> 00:09:22,658
Maybury's yoga takes twenty-five.

224
00:09:22,764 --> 00:09:24,442
Neither one needs a crowd.

225
00:09:24,599 --> 00:09:26,598
It needs a date and a way to pay.

226
00:09:26,883 --> 00:09:28,564
Before you sell one ticket,

227
00:09:28,584 --> 00:09:31,160
make three calls. Your insurer.

228
00:09:31,334 --> 00:09:33,047
Your county zoning office.

229
00:09:33,198 --> 00:09:35,814
And your health department,
if there's food.

230
00:09:35,873 --> 00:09:37,413
Rules differ by place,

231
00:09:37,433 --> 00:09:38,723
so ask first.

232
00:09:39,017 --> 00:09:42,066
Go back to that February yard. Same mud.

233
00:09:42,156 --> 00:09:43,576
Same quiet phone.

234
00:09:44,683 --> 00:09:46,842
But now there's a date on the calendar.

235
00:09:47,004 --> 00:09:48,427
A table in the barn.

236
00:09:48,611 --> 00:09:50,104
Ten cars at the gate.

237
00:09:50,417 --> 00:09:53,324
Watching won't change
anything. Doing will.

238
00:09:53,617 --> 00:09:54,912
Here's your homework.

239
00:09:55,058 --> 00:09:56,538
What's your quietest month,

240
00:09:56,558 --> 00:09:58,502
and which of these thirty would you run?

241
00:09:58,702 --> 00:10:00,103
Tell me in the comments.

242
